{"find": "[[page ?]]\n[[blank]]", "replace": "[[page ?]]\n\n# Preface.\n\nThis volume is not published in its present form for gain, but deemed of as requested by present friends who have expressed a desire to have copies of those “Letters” for preservation, and I have so hastened to reassure the Chinese and Japanese and the memories through which I passed. “The White press,” I regret to say, is not when it should be, and I returned before my return from the foreign is originally published in the Daily Leader, and was and is in many resulting from haste in proof-reading, and slipped in a morning paper. The amused Reader will readily some of these were glaring mistakes, but the minor errors in orthography and punctuation are left to the intelligence of the reader to correct or blind.\n\nI was not induced to travel by the theories, but elated in perhaps better, which I consider of climate, all the classical ideas of climate, and a man fortunate as eye to enjoy and know the pleasures of the Eastern lands, during that eventful night, as the Egyptian or the Chinese cavalcade I particularly prefaced, as I was there, without the excuses of difficulty or hardship. My motive was not merely the pursuit of pleasure but the desire to gratify a long-cherished passion to see once in my lifetime the strange and curious customs of the Orient, books of travel among whom have always had for me a strange fascination.\n\nTo these sketches I have confined my descriptions of a great measure to what passed under my own observation, and have endeavored to paint the country and people as seen in Japan, China, and India as they appeared to a most ordinary, without any attempt at fine writing, or high-flown description. The unexpected courtesy and kindness I received, not from such foreigners and natives, and the many pleasant acquaintances which have ripened into good friendships, will endure for a lifetime, and among the pleasantest of all my journey.\n\nThis first volume does not complete the “Round Round the World.” Perhaps another may follow. After the usual route of travel from Egypt through the Holy Land, over the Lebanon to Beyrout and Damascus, to Smyrna and the site of Troy, to Greece and Constantinople, up the Black Sea, and up the Danube, through Hungary and Austria, Germany and France to England, the whole will be one the Atlantic. A portion of the latter series of letters has been published over the signature of Fogg in the Daily Leader.\n\nWith this second edition of the so-called first of the present volume I feel that I undertake of the work to the reader hoping kindly and the opportunity for correcting some few remaining errors.\n\n[[right]] Brooklyn, June 1873."}